[{:seon.task/name "a-contract-gap-is-fixed-not-repeated"
  :seon.task/plan "Agents define helpers without :malli/schema and keep doing so after the done rule refuses (T5 real run 3: t5.refusal/describe capped at 6 turns refused for contract gaps on t5.refusal/bounds, describe*, join-or). Make the fix obvious at the producer: after a contract-gap refusal the context (`seon.context/current-state`) names each uncontracted var with the one-line form that contracts it. Done when a scripted agent that leaves a helper uncontracted fixes it on its next turn from the context alone. The proof is not written."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/a-contract-gap-names-each-var-and-its-fix}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-agent-sees-what-the-judge-sees"
  :seon.task/plan "The context is built from the turn's whole observation (`seon.turn/observe`): the done rule's errors, test-run errors that name no var (a timeout or dead test fork, which once read as :none), the reader's repairs, and flagged definitions reach `seon.context/current-state`, so an agent cannot loop to its cap with every visible proof green. Tests run program namespaces and proofs only, never scratch."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/a-reason-and-a-run-error-reach-the-context
                      seon.context-test/a-repair-is-shown-next-turn}}
 {:seon.task/name "last-evals-fold-and-are-budgeted"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: `seon.context/fold-evals` folds identical form and answer into one item with :times n; `seon.context/shown-evals` gives each folded item an equal share of `:seon.config.render/evals-budget` (8000), every cut naming `(nth (my.turn/evals) k)`, with :out printed inside the item's share; a failure reads as {:assert form :got value}. The last evaluations are turn content, sent after the stable prefix. Why: every measured context overrun was last-evals (one turn 259 items, 11 distinct, 101k tokens uncut; `(pp/pprint (code-of 10000))` x3 = 10.7k tokens of :out). Open from the plan: red proofs' program callees shown one level."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/repeated-evals-fold-and-every-item-fits-its-share
                      seon.context-test/a-failure-reads-as-assert-and-got}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-standard-repl-tools"
  :seon.task/plan "The agent's REPL answers what a Clojure REPL answers (DECISIONS: standard REPL experience, optimized for Jolt): `source` (`my.agent/source-fn`: the branch first, then a library var's :file and :line), `pst` (`my.agent/pst`), and *1 *2 *3 *e per session (`seon.repl/eval-code`), proven by `seon.repl-test/an-agent-repl-answers-what-a-clojure-repl-answers`; definitions come from the compiler (`seon.program/tap`). What remains: doc's bounded see-also (contract, callers, callees, reaching tests, tasks, branch vs main), whose functions exist as `my.agent/callers` and `my.agent/callees`; and this task's three listed proofs, none of which is written (the merged behaviour is proven by the test above). Subsumes seon.repl/source-at-the-repl."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/source-shows-the-exact-definition-mid-request
                      seon.repl-test/doc-names-callers-callees-and-reaching-tests
                      seon.repl-test/star-1-and-star-e-hold-the-last-value-and-error}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-accumulated-plan-names-whose-plan-it-is"
  :seon.task/plan "A task's context shows the accumulated plan (`seon.task/accumulate` over :needs, by design) as paragraphs with nothing saying which task each came from, so a needed task's paragraphs read as this task's; its proofs list every needed task's tests (the done rule judges all of them) with no marker of whose each is. Reproduction: attach to `seon.source/merges-are-committed-and-run-next` and read `{:op :context}`: four plan paragraphs, seven proofs, one of them its own. Correct: each accumulated paragraph and proof carries the task it came from (its id), this task's own first; nothing else about accumulation changes. The render is `seon.context/current-state`. The proof is not written."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/an-accumulated-plan-names-whose-plan-it-is}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-stable-prefix-is-sent-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: `seon.context/messages` sends [system: the help page, docs/agent.md, the same for every agent] [user: the attempt's sections (task, plan, references, found helpers, sections, evidence), rendered from the attempt's first turn's basis] [the scrollback] [user: the turn's sections (turn n of m, proofs, refused, test-run, last reply, unmerged entries, last evaluations, the dropped-turns note)]. The first two messages are byte-identical across an attempt's turns and nothing is sent twice; DeepSeek and OpenAI-compatible providers cache a byte-identical prefix. Replaced ``seon.context/system-prompt`` and the overflow note's edit of the first message (before, the whole block went out twice a turn and the prefix changed every turn after overflow; notes/archive/AUDIT-v1-to-next-2026-10-22.md 2.3). v1 sent stable attempt sections in the system message (about 8.5k tokens) and small turn sections (about 0.75k), with 85-91% cache hits. `seon.context/render` stays the one maker; `seon.turn/context-block` shows both user messages."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/the-prefix-stays-byte-identical-across-turns
                      seon.context-test/each-section-is-sent-once-per-request}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-cited-var-shows-at-its-citation-depth"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: `seon.context/resolve-ref` shows a cited var at the depth its delimiter asks (docs/references.md#the-delimiter-picks-the-depth, from `seon.ref/references`): bare is its summary (arglists, the docstring's first sentence), one backtick its doc (arglists, docstring, contract), three its source; a source over `:seon.config.render/reference-tokens` shows the doc instead, saying how many tokens it left out and naming `(clojure.repl/source ns/f)`. Before, every cited var showed its whole source (a real task's context was 17 KB, 8.9 KB of it source; notes/archive/LEARNING-2026-10-22.md). v1 capped a must-read at `reference-tokens` 3000 with the form that reads the rest."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/a-cited-var-shows-at-its-citations-depth
                      seon.context-test/a-source-over-the-cap-says-what-it-left-and-how-to-read-it}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-context-names-the-helpers-a-task-needs"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: `seon.context/found` gives the attempt's sections `:found`, the program functions that the plan's cited vars and the task's proofs call (one hop through `:form/refs`, `my.agent/callees`) and that neither the plan nor a red proof's `:calls` names, each at doc depth, ranked by how many of those sources call them, at most `:seon.config.render/found` (5), then a line saying how many it left. Why: R4 (notes/archive/option3-R4-learning.md sections 0, 5, 7.2) found a context naming the helpers took 8 tasks from 7/32 green to 22/24, turns p50 3 to 1, tokens -69%. Not covered: a helper nothing calls yet (R4's case), which needs the embedding index (the `:embed` effect); its hits join the same list fused by rank (DECISIONS: RRF)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/found-names-the-helpers-the-plan-does-not}}
 {:seon.task/name "help-is-a-checked-docs-page"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: docs/agent.md is the help page, a wiki page like any other; the system message is that page; `my.agent/help` answers its sections (address, heading, lead), and one section whole by anchor; `seon.repl-test/the-help-page-runs` runs every single-backticked form on the page in a real agent, in order, and every name it cites resolves. Replaced ``seon.context/system-prompt`` (prose in code, checked by nothing) and `seon.template/repl`'s navigation forms. v1's `(help)` was sectioned data whose every reference resolved, tested (~/src/seon/test/seon/bootstrap_test.clj)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/the-help-page-runs
                      seon.repl-test/help-answers-a-section}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-turn-shows-what-will-merge"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: `seon.program/divergence` names the vars the branch added, redefined and removed and the schema keys it registered or changed (from each db's :var/form and :schema/form datoms; `seon.program/changed-vars` is its vars); `seon.turn/observe` takes it against the agent's `:base` (`seon.agent/state`), its branch's merge base with main, set at init and by `seon.agent/merge-main!` after each merge (and for a resumed branch), and `seon.context/current-state` shows it as `:will-merge` among the turn sections (DECISIONS: D4). The merge base, not the main the agent forked from: against that, a branch cut before main changed a function showed main's change as its own. Not covered: data entities a branch writes (`seon.merge/program`'s :datum keys) and multimethod and protocol definitions, which only the full program merge sees (`seon.merge/program`, 440 ms per db, too slow for every turn)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/divergence-names-each-change-a-merge-carries
                      seon.context-test/the-turn-names-what-the-merge-will-carry}}
 {:seon.task/name "similar-finds-helpers-by-embedding"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the context's `:found` names helpers by the call graph (`seon.context/found`), which finds a helper something already calls; `my.program/search` and `similar` are missing (docs/parity.md#the-agents-repl-my). The ruling is DECISIONS \"Embeddings join the context\": fused, small and measured. Earlier work exists only outside this repository: `~/src/seon-jolt/em1` (a brute-force kNN in C, `knn.c` and `csrc/proto_knn.c`, 10k x 768 in 0.39 ms; the embed service in `server` and `src`; `patches/agents-01-embed.patch` and `core-01-embed.patch`; measurements and the R@10 corpus in `out/`), recorded in the outer repository's notes/archive/option3-EM1-embeddings.md; R4 measured the lever: 7/32 green without helper naming, 22/24 with. What correct looks like: an embedding per program function kept with its form, a kNN over them through the effect owner, `similar` at the REPL and the top hits beside `:found` in the context, each measured (recall on the corpus, cost per context build). Model downloads are an open owner question (RESUME)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/similar-finds-a-helper-the-call-graph-misses}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-turn-shows-how-main-diverged"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `:will-merge` (`seon.program/divergence` against the agent's merge base) shows what the branch carries; nothing shows what main changed since that base, so a coming conflict is met only at merge. Correct (DESIGN-tasks §3.6, prototyped): one producer of main's changes since the base intersected with the branch's, shown every turn as {:main-ahead n :conflict [..] :you-call [..] :other n} (about 30 tokens): changed by both, changed on main and called by the branch's changes (:form/refs), the rest counted. Its cost follows the changes since the base, not the program (0.4-0.6 ms with a changed-keys index; a full scan 31 ms per db), so it needs the changed-since primitive of seon.merge/program-cost-follows-what-changed."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.merge/program-cost-follows-what-changed"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/a-turn-names-mains-coming-conflict}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-unrun-proof-is-unknown-in-the-context"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.context` labels a proof with no result :no-assertions unless the run failed as a whole (the :proofs entry, `(get results p (cond ... :else :no-assertions))`), and docs/agent.md#what-you-read tells the agent that means it ran and asserted nothing. A result is absent whenever no report named the test (`seon.agent/run-tests-forked`): one not selected or not loaded as well as one with no assertion. Root's judge reads the same absence as :proof-unknown (`seon.reconcile/done-explain`), so agent and judge disagree on one fact. Reproduction (audit): 263 of 265 contexts showed every proof :no-assertions while none had run; agents then loosened a contract. Correct: the context says :unknown, the judge's word. Deletes: the :no-assertions default."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/a-proof-with-no-result-reads-unknown}}
 {:seon.task/name "old-evaluations-collapse-in-chunks"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: past `:seon.config.render/history-budget`, `seon.context/kept-turns` drops the oldest turn on every turn, so the message after the attempt sections changes each request and the scrollback misses the provider's prefix cache from then on. Field: SWE-agent's collapse of all but the last 5 observations beat full history by 3 points; Anthropic reports clearing old tool results as the lightest compaction; Manus keeps the context append-only. Correct: at the boundary, collapse the evaluations of all turns but the last N (a config key) to one line each (the form, its status, `(nth (my.turn/evals) k)`), once, so the collapsed prefix stays fixed for the following turns. Measure: cache-hit share per turn from turn 8 on, and turns to green on long attempts."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/the-collapsed-prefix-is-stable-across-turns}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-turn-message-stays-in-the-scrollback"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.context/messages` rebuilds the turn sections every turn as the last user message and drops the earlier ones, so request k+1 is request k minus its last message. DeepSeek makes cache units at the end of each request's input and output, so the newest scrollback turn sits on no unit and hits only through the lazy shared-prefix units, about one turn behind (~5-8k missed tokens a turn, estimated). Correct: each turn's state message stays in the history after that turn's tool messages (made again from its basis, `seon.context/turn-text`); the newest is the current state; older ones may show as a diff against the one before. Prove the prefix stays byte-identical when rendered in two processes (the printer's map order). Measure: hit share per turn on the scripted dry run; v1 got >85%."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/request-k-plus-1-starts-with-request-k-whole}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-red-proofs-calls-are-its-recorded-refs"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: two producers of a function's callees. `my.agent/callees` reads :form/refs (the compiler's facts); `seon.context/callees` (context.clj:101-115), which fills a red proof's :calls (:250), re-reads the proof's source with seon.reader and ns-resolves every symbol in it, so a quoted list or a local named like a var counts as a call. Correct: seon.context/callees is the :form/refs query, kept to program vars that are not tests. Deletes the read-and-resolve."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/a-red-proofs-calls-are-its-recorded-refs}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-map-reads-the-program-not-the-process"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the review of jolt/dry-run (2026-10-23): ```seon.context/ns-map``` takes each var's arglists from the running process (`find-ns`, `ns-interns`) while its summary comes from the recorded form (```seon.context/summary-of```), so one db rendered in two processes (root, an agent whose branch redefined a var) gives two texts, and the context is meant to be a render of the db value. ```seon.context/summary-of``` too reads the runtime doc first and the recorded form only when there is none. Correct: read both the arglists and the summary from the recorded form, so `ns-map` is a function of db alone. Proof: the map of a db holding a var whose form differs from the process's (its arglists and its docstring) shows the form's arglists and summary."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/the-map-reads-the-program-not-the-process}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-state-names-what-was-read"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by step E's runs #8 and #9 (2026-10-23, DeepSeek flash, thinking off; attempts seon.context/e1b-map-from-program#1 and e2b-map-from-program#1 on worktree dry-run's root, mains e1 and e2): a design read the same sources turn after turn (`clojure.repl/source` of ns-map in turns 0, 6, 11, 12 of run #8; turns 4-6 of run #9) though every result stood in its scrollback, until the no-progress end. Correct: the current state (```seon.context/current-state```) records what this attempt has already read (each name and the turn that read it, from the recorded evals), so a re-read is visible as one before the attempt ends. Recording, never a gate: nothing refuses a read. Proof: the state of an attempt whose turns read seon.context/ns-map twice names it with both turns."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/the-state-names-what-was-read}} {:seon.task/name "evaluations"
  :seon.task/plan "The model's context is a sequence of real evaluations, rendered from their records by the one printer's :ai face; no hand-built sections (owner, 2026-10-23: \"stick with a system message that we put in via (help) and then a pull and walk render in simple clojure code so there's no magic and we output data for the responses for the agent and it should be cache stable and grow with transcript size mostly except the dynamic portions which should be at the end\"; \"everything is structured as separate evals where we show the forms the agent input (or we generate the help and agents entity walk) and they can clearly see how results come back with timing and individual renders being called so they can see what functions are rendering what data and where the data is so they can update it to update their context generation intuitively\"; \"everything is real and is structured just like the model ran them and recorded and rendered with render functions not manual wiring\"; \"These need to be cachable so we aren't literatlly re-running things\"; \"now can be a named function\"). Root's ruling (decisions.md, 2026-10-23): no live agent runs until the context has a format agents respond well to; each version is scored by agent-loop's judge (about 10 real task contexts, the first move scored blind) and read by Haiku readers (notes/context-reader.md, from one request: {:op :context :task}); keep each change small enough to score often. What exists: seon.context/render builds the system message and the current-state block from hand-built sections (seon.context/current-state, sections, request-text), seon.turn/basis! records a turn's basis, and the context is remade from it. Build, in order, each step scored: (1) the system message is (help)'s text (my.agent/help), stable: markdown, a doc like any wiki page, whose citations seon.ref/parse reads and the context pulls as it pulls any page's (owner, 2026-10-23: \"If help is best a markdown style response and then we parse it for refs that works better I think\"); it says plainly what the views are made of: the walk, the one printer, each value's declared render, and the page's Tailwind (classes in hiccup, regenerated by bin/build), and it cites the docs an agent needs (docs/render.md, docs/references.md for the citation format, the Tailwind setup) at the depth each should be pulled, so they reach every context through the citation mechanism (owner, 2026-10-23: \"Make sure the help text is clear that we are using tailwind and put any refs in there that need ot be pulled in to documentation\"); (2) turn 0 is evaluations the turn loop makes and records as the model's are (origin :context): (help)'s sections it needs, then (my.agent/walk) of the agent's own entity (seon.render/walk: agent, run, task, plan, the plan's citations at their depth, the proofs and how each proof's db is made, from its namespace's fixtures: agent-loop's step E run 11, the model never found them); (3) each later turn appends the model's calls: each evaluation's ;; thought, form, result by the result's own :ai render, and its cost as recorded when it ran (ticks, ms, bytes, reused or computed: seon.turn/a-turn-shows-its-cost-against-its-budget); a recorded evaluation is rendered from its record and never run again, so every earlier turn renders the same bytes and the provider's cache holds (a cost proof: the prefix is byte-identical across turns, and the context grows with the transcript); (4) the last evaluation, each turn, is (my.agent/now): proofs now, the inbox, what this run has read already (task the-state-names-what-was-read), the one part that breaks the cache; (5) the citations a string holds are shown as citations with what they resolved to, so an agent learns that writing seon.ref's format in any string field brings the cited data in; (6) which render made which part shows as the measured readers find useful, not more (owner: \"mostly helpful data not just a shitload of data\"). Deletes seon.context's section builders (current-state, sections, the request assembly) as each step replaces them; agent-loop's found and ns-map go when the walk's citations replace them (agent-loop: nothing of theirs depends on them)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.render/walk"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/turn-zero-is-recorded-evaluations seon.context-test/a-recorded-evaluation-renders-the-same-bytes seon.context-test/now-is-the-last-evaluation seon.context-cost-test/the-context-grows-with-the-transcript}}
]
